  "verbalization": "Levorphanol is linked to Oprk1 through a series of molecular and pharmacological relationships that converge on the opioid receptor agonist class. The evidence suggests that Levorphanol, through its structural resemblance to Levallorphan and Pentazocine, binds to Oprk1, a key component of the opioid receptor system. This is further supported by the fact that Levorphanol and Levallorphan both belong to the Morphinane Alkaloid class, which is a well-known group of compounds that interact with opioid receptors. The shared pathway from Levorphanol to Oprk1 involves a series of intermediate steps, including binding to opioid receptors and being classified as organonitrogen compounds or agents affecting the nervous system. These connections highlight the role of Levorphanol as a member of the opioid receptor agonist family, with a potential mechanism of action involving the modulation of Oprk1, which is critical for the physiological effects of opioid drugs. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of Levorphanol in the broader context of opioid receptor activation and its potential therapeutic applications.",
